        "explanation": "In Python, an object is Hashable if it has a hash value that never changes during its lifetime (requires `__hash__()`) and can be compared to other objects for equality (requires `__eq__()`).\n\nThe Hash Contract Rule:\nIf two objects are considered equal (`a == b`), they MUST have the same hash value (`hash(a) == hash(b)`).\nThe reverse is NOT necessarily true: two different objects can have the same hash value (a hash collision).\n\nWhy Mutable Objects Cannot Be Hashed:\nIf a mutable object (like a list or dict) were permitted as a dictionary key or set item, mutating that object would change its hash value. The object would then reside in the wrong bucket of the hash table, becoming completely unfindable or corrupting table invariants. Hence, CPython sets `__hash__ = None` on mutable containers, raising `TypeError: unhashable type: 'list'`.\n\nCustom Classes:\nBy default, custom class instances use their memory identity (`id()`) for hashing and equality. If you override `__eq__()`, Python automatically unsets `__hash__` unless you explicitly implement a custom `__hash__()` method.",
