        "explanation": "Python's `with` statement manages the deterministic acquisition and release of resources (file descriptors, database transactions, mutex locks, network sockets).\n\nThe Context Manager Protocol:\nAny object implementing two dunder methods conforms to the Context Manager Protocol:\n1. `__enter__(self)`: Executes before the with-block begins. Its return value is bound to the identifier after `as` (`with Resource() as r:`).\n2. `__exit__(self, exc_type, exc_val, exc_tb)`: Executes when control exits the with-block, whether normally or due to an unhandled exception.\n   - If an exception occurred, `exc_type`, `exc_val`, and `exc_tb` hold the exception details.\n   - If `__exit__` returns `True`, the exception is SUPPRESSED (swallowed).\n   - If it returns `False` (or None), the exception is re-raised automatically.\n\nThe `@contextlib.contextmanager` Utility:\nAllows creating context managers using a generator with a single `yield` statement enclosed in a `try...finally` block, eliminating class boilerplate.",
