        "explanation": "The Global Interpreter Lock (GIL) is a mutual exclusion mutex in CPython that prevents multiple native OS threads from executing Python bytecode simultaneously.\n\nWhy Does the GIL Exist?\nCPython's memory management relies on Reference Counting. Without the GIL, concurrent threads mutating reference counts (`ob_refcnt`) would cause catastrophic race conditions, memory leaks, and memory corruption.\n\nImplications for Engineers:\n1. I/O-Bound Workloads (network calls, database queries, file reads):\n   The GIL is RELEASED during blocking I/O calls (such as `socket.recv()`, `time.sleep()`, or database driver waits). Therefore, Python Multithreading (`threading`) or Asyncio is HIGHLY EFFECTIVE for I/O-bound tasks.\n2. CPU-Bound Workloads (cryptography, image processing, matrix multiplication, ML training):\n   OS threads fight for the single GIL, causing thread context-switching overhead and running SLOWER than a single thread. CPU-bound workloads must use Multiprocessing (`multiprocessing`), bypassing the GIL with separate OS processes!\n(Note: PEP 703 introduces free-threaded Python 3.13 where the GIL can optionally be disabled, but standard production runtimes remain GIL-governed).",
