        "explanation": "When multiple threads share mutable memory (e.g. updating a bank balance or modifying a dictionary), Race Conditions occur when the outcome depends on arbitrary thread scheduling.\n\nWhy Race Conditions Happen Even with the GIL:\nThe GIL guarantees that individual bytecode instructions are atomic, but high-level Python operations (like `balance += amount`) compile to MULTIPLE bytecode instructions:\n1. `LOAD_GLOBAL (balance)`\n2. `LOAD_FAST (amount)`\n3. `INPLACE_ADD`\n4. `STORE_GLOBAL (balance)`\nCPython can switch thread execution context between any of these opcodes, resulting in corrupted data!\n\nThread Synchronization Tools:\n1. `threading.Lock`: Mutual exclusion primitive. A thread acquires the lock before entering critical sections and releases it when done (idiomatically using `with lock:`).\n2. `threading.RLock` (Reentrant Lock): Can be acquired multiple times by the SAME thread without deadlocking.\n3. `queue.Queue`: Thread-safe FIFO queue with built-in locking and condition variables, ideal for Producer-Consumer patterns.",
